1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,259
You got the grant.

2
00:00:01,259 --> 00:00:03,008
Now you have to spend it.

3
00:00:03,008 --> 00:00:06,507
And at some point, someone asks a simple question.

4
00:00:06,507 --> 00:00:10,565
How much of this grant is left, and where did the rest go?

5
00:00:10,565 --> 00:00:12,873
Your bank balance won't tell you.

6
00:00:12,873 --> 00:00:17,701
One account holds every grant and your own money, all mixed together.

7
00:00:17,701 --> 00:00:21,059
Today I'll show you how to track grant spending.

8
00:00:21,059 --> 00:00:24,558
You'll be able to answer that any day of the week.

9
00:00:24,558 --> 00:00:26,307
Without losing your mind.

10
00:00:26,307 --> 00:00:28,825
Quick note on where I'm coming from.

11
00:00:28,825 --> 00:00:31,204
I build grant compliance software.

12
00:00:31,204 --> 00:00:34,633
I'm not a former grants officer or an accountant.

13
00:00:34,633 --> 00:00:36,172
I won't pretend to be.

14
00:00:36,172 --> 00:00:39,250
You're getting the version a busy director needs.

15
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:42,995
So what does tracking grant spending really mean?

16
00:00:42,995 --> 00:00:46,358
Every dollar you spend belongs to one grant.

17
00:00:46,358 --> 00:00:49,339
The number that matters is what's left.

18
00:00:49,339 --> 00:00:51,021
Take the award amount.

19
00:00:51,021 --> 00:00:53,925
Subtract what you've spent against it.

20
00:00:53,925 --> 00:00:56,830
What's left is your remaining balance.

21
00:00:56,830 --> 00:00:57,747
Simple math.

22
00:00:57,747 --> 00:01:01,798
It only works if each cost points to the right grant.

23
00:01:01,798 --> 00:01:05,620
Get it wrong in two directions, and both cost you.

24
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:10,588
Spend on something the grant doesn't cover, and you eat the cost.

25
00:01:10,588 --> 00:01:12,881
Go over the award, same thing.

26
00:01:12,881 --> 00:01:16,932
Spend too slowly, and the funder takes the rest back.

27
00:01:16,932 --> 00:01:19,072
Your next grant shrinks too.

28
00:01:19,072 --> 00:01:22,620
A clear remaining number keeps both from happening.

29
00:01:22,620 --> 00:01:24,303
It takes three things.

30
00:01:24,303 --> 00:01:28,127
A running total for each grant that you can trust.

31
00:01:28,127 --> 00:01:31,799
A list of every cost, so you can show your work.

32
00:01:31,799 --> 00:01:36,464
And a sense of pace, so the deadline doesn't sneak up on you.

33
00:01:36,464 --> 00:01:39,294
The way this falls apart is familiar.

34
00:01:39,294 --> 00:01:40,901
One checking account.

35
00:01:40,901 --> 00:01:42,507
Receipts in a drawer.

36
00:01:42,507 --> 00:01:43,884
No per-grant view.

37
00:01:43,884 --> 00:01:46,408
You feel fine until month eleven.

38
00:01:46,408 --> 00:01:48,167
Then you add it all up.

39
00:01:48,167 --> 00:01:51,762
You're over on one line, and behind on another.

40
00:01:51,762 --> 00:01:53,630
By then it's too late to fix.

41
00:01:53,630 --> 00:01:57,980
Let me show you one way to do this, in the real app.

42
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:02,665
Open a grant, and the four numbers sit right at the top.

43
00:02:02,665 --> 00:02:05,092
Grant Amount, the full award.

44
00:02:05,092 --> 00:02:09,275
Allocated, how much you've assigned to your funds.

45
00:02:09,275 --> 00:02:11,115
Unallocated, the rest.

46
00:02:11,115 --> 00:02:15,298
And the one you'll watch most, Remaining to Spend.

47
00:02:15,298 --> 00:02:18,896
That's the live answer to how much is left.

48
00:02:18,896 --> 00:02:20,653
You don't type it in.

49
00:02:20,653 --> 00:02:26,760
The app takes the award and subtracts every expense charged to the grant.

50
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:29,840
Spend more, and it ticks down on its own.

51
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:32,405
How does it know what you spent?

52
00:02:32,405 --> 00:02:35,051
You tell it, on the Expenses tab.

53
00:02:35,051 --> 00:02:37,617
This is every cost on the grant.

54
00:02:37,617 --> 00:02:41,304
Each line shows a short note and what it cost.

55
00:02:41,304 --> 00:02:44,030
To add one, you open a small form.

56
00:02:44,030 --> 00:02:46,756
Amount, date, a quick description.

57
00:02:46,756 --> 00:02:47,558
That's it.

58
00:02:47,558 --> 00:02:50,043
Save it, and two things happen.

59
00:02:50,043 --> 00:02:55,415
The cost joins the list, and Remaining to Spend drops by that much.

60
00:02:55,415 --> 00:03:00,225
So the list and the running total stay in step on their own.

61
00:03:00,225 --> 00:03:03,111
That list is what you hand a funder.

62
00:03:03,111 --> 00:03:07,010
When they ask where the money went, it's right there.

63
00:03:07,010 --> 00:03:09,045
Now the part that saves you.

64
00:03:09,045 --> 00:03:09,409
Pace.

65
00:03:09,409 --> 00:03:11,662
The grant shows your burn rate.

66
00:03:11,662 --> 00:03:15,587
That's just how much you spend each month, on average.

67
00:03:15,587 --> 00:03:17,913
That one number tells you a lot.

68
00:03:17,913 --> 00:03:21,983
At this rate, will you land near zero by the close date?

69
00:03:21,983 --> 00:03:23,219
Or run out early?

70
00:03:23,219 --> 00:03:26,199
Or sit on money you'll have to give back?

71
00:03:26,199 --> 00:03:28,525
The Spend-Down view lays it out.

72
00:03:28,525 --> 00:03:30,269
You see the drift early.

73
00:03:30,269 --> 00:03:32,304
Early enough to still steer.

74
00:03:32,304 --> 00:03:33,104
Quick note.

75
00:03:33,104 --> 00:03:36,593
That view comes with the larger GrantPipe plans.

76
00:03:36,593 --> 00:03:38,060
Check that it's on yours.

77
00:03:38,060 --> 00:03:39,778
So that's the whole job.

78
00:03:39,778 --> 00:03:44,289
Tracking grant spending comes down to one number you can trust.

79
00:03:44,289 --> 00:03:46,151
What's left on this grant.

80
00:03:46,151 --> 00:03:50,733
Tie every cost to its grant, keep the list, and watch your pace.

81
00:03:50,733 --> 00:03:53,382
Do that, and report time gets boring.

82
00:03:53,382 --> 00:03:55,458
That's exactly what you want.

83
00:03:55,458 --> 00:03:58,895
A spreadsheet can carry this while you're small.

84
00:03:58,895 --> 00:04:02,045
A system carries it for you when you're not.

85
00:04:02,045 --> 00:04:05,912
That's the kind of thing we built GrantPipe to handle.

86
00:04:05,912 --> 00:04:08,847
But the habit matters more than the tool.

87
00:04:08,847 --> 00:04:10,422
Want a starting point?

88
00:04:10,422 --> 00:04:12,356
We made a free spreadsheet.

89
00:04:12,356 --> 00:04:14,718
It sets up these columns for you.

90
00:04:14,718 --> 00:04:16,723
We'll send it to your inbox.

91
00:04:16,723 --> 00:04:21,377
Next up, how to keep this straight across a dozen grants at once.

92
00:04:21,377 --> 00:04:25,100
Thanks for watching, and good luck spending it down.
